== Wire-order bug found and fixed (the audit working as designed) ==
Weft.kt wBegin()/rLiveBuf(): Java ByteBuffer.slice() does NOT inherit the
source's LITTLE_ENDIAN order — the payload slices came back BIG_ENDIAN
over the little-endian wire buffer, silently byte-swapping every u32/f32
word written/read through them (byte-granular users unaffected — which is
why the roundtrip test never caught it: it swapped BOTH ends). Caught by
R7's word-level parity check; both slices now pin the order explicitly;
WeftTest roundtrip re-verified green.

== Drawing-loop allocation stripping (the audit's findings) ==
- Kotlin Weft.rLiveBuf(): 2 ByteBuffer wrappers per call (duplicate +
  slice) — documented as the cold-path API; the per-frame consumer API is
  now rLiveWords() (zero allocation, R8-proven).
- Kotlin fanout view(), Swift rLivePtr(), Dart claim()/view(): already
  zero-alloc (verified — no strip needed).
- The producer-side wBegin() wrapper cost is a WRITER-thread concern,
  declared out of the drawing-loop audit's scope (R8 is per-thread).

